The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
tve->ddc = of_find_i2c_adapter_by_node(ddc_node);
```
Reason:
`of_find_i2c_adapter_by_node()` returns an i2c_adapter with an elevated reference count (via `get_device()`). The driver stores this reference in `tve->ddc` but never calls `put_device()` on `tve->ddc->dev` when probe fails (e.g., due to `-EINVAL` or other errors) or when the driver is unbound. This causes a device reference leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+static void imx_tve_put_device(void *_dev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = _dev;
+
+	put_device(dev);
+}
...
+		if (tve->ddc) {
+			ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, imx_tve_put_device,
+						       &tve->ddc->dev);
+			if (ret)
+				return ret;
+		}
```
Reason:
The `devm_add_action_or_reset()` registers a cleanup action that calls `put_device()` on the DDC device when the driver is removed or if probe fails after this point. The helper `imx_tve_put_device` simply calls `put_device()` on the passed device pointer. This ensures the reference is always released, fixing the leak.

